# PROVENANCE: measured, not generated. Every response byte below was captured
# from a genuine DESFire EV1 4K over PC/SC with opensc-tool and is used here as
# an expectation for our emulator. As with hardware_genuine_ev1_4k.vec, this
# vector is the opposite of the rest of the corpus: the others were recorded
# from the reference engine, so they pin what we decided; this one pins what
# the silicon does. A mismatch here is either a real divergence or a difference
# we can justify in writing, never a vector to be re-recorded from our engine.
#
# Card: UID 045704EA916F80, hardware 1.0, software 1.4, storage 4K. Same
# physical card as hardware_genuine_ev1_4k.vec, but now carrying a real issued
# credential rather than a hand-built test structure: application D3494F (wire
# order 4F49D3) with an ISO file ID of E110 and no DF name, four keys of the DES
# family, and one Standard Data file 0F of 54 octets under MACed communication
# with asymmetric access rights.
#
# What this vector is for: the whole unauthenticated surface of an issued
# credential. It pins the access rights of a real credential against silicon,
# which is the direct check on the file-access-rights nibble order, and it pins
# the status the card gives for an unauthenticated read of a MACed file.
#
# NOT captured, deliberately: GetKeyVersion. On this card the keys are DES
# family, whose version byte is the concatenation of the parity bits of the key
# itself. The keys here are production keys, so their version bytes are a
# function of secret material and must not enter this tree. The card does answer
# GetKeyVersion without authentication; only the values are withheld. What is
# safe and is captured is the refusal for a key number the application does not
# have.

name: genuine EV1 4K, issued credential read-only surface
origin: physical-card
feature-version: 5
uid: 045704EA916F80
picc-storage: 8192
picc-key-settings-1: 0F
picc-key-settings-2: 01
picc-auth-mode: D40
picc-key-len: 16
picc-hardware-version: 04010101001A05
picc-software-version: 04010101041A05

scan

# PICC key settings, unchanged by issuance: the credential records no PICC key,
# so the card keeps the master key and the permission set it already had.
exchange native-apdu 9045000000 => 0F019100

# Build the issued structure. Free create is permitted by PICC key settings 0F,
# so CreateApplication needs no authentication. KS2 is sent as 24: 04 for four
# DES-family keys, plus bit 5 to declare the ISO file ID that follows.
#
# The application's own KS1 as measured on the card is 0B. It is reconstructed
# here as 0F, for the reason set out under the suspected divergence below; the
# measured value is recorded in that note and is not asserted, because the
# response that carries it is already unexecuted under the KS2 divergence below.
status 90CA0000074F49D30F2410E1 => 00

# The application directory, least significant byte first on the wire.
exchange native-apdu 906A000000 => 4F49D39100

# GetDFNames. The rule is "list applications that have an ISO file ID", not
# "list applications that have a DF name": the AID and the file ID are emitted
# with the DF name simply absent, which is this application's case.
exchange native-apdu 906D000000 => 4F49D310E19100

# Select it. Its key settings are its own and are not inherited from the PICC.
status 905A0000034F49D300 => 00

# Key settings. CreateApplication was sent KS2 24: 04 for four DES-family keys,
# plus bit 5 to declare the ISO file ID that follows. That bit is a modifier on
# the command and not state, so KS2 reads back as 04.
#
# KS1 is wildcarded: the captured octet was 0B, which the issuer chose, while the
# structure here is reconstructed with 0F. The KS2 octet is the measurement.
exchange native-apdu 9045000000 => ?? 049100

# CLOSED, was a suspected divergence: free file creation under application
# KS1 0B.
#
# This note used to read our engine's 91 AE for an unauthenticated
# CreateStdDataFile in an application reconstructed with KS1 0B as a sign that
# we do not honour the application-level free-create bit. It has since been
# measured, in hardware_genuine_ev1_4k_free_create.vec, and there is no
# divergence: the bit was misidentified. Free create and delete is bit 2, not
# bit 1, and KS1 0B has bit 2 clear. The card refuses the same command in an
# application whose KS1 is 02 and accepts it when the KS1 is 04, so 0B really
# does require an authentication and our 91 AE was correct.
#
# The application is still reconstructed above with KS1 0F rather than the
# measured 0B, because the response that would carry 0B is unexecuted under the
# KS2 divergence above and 0F is what lets this vector reach the file settings
# it exists to pin.
#
# The credential's file. Created with an ISO file ID of 000F, MACed
# communication, and access rights 0x1230, which is 30 12 on the wire.
status 90CD0000090F0F00013012360000 => 00

exchange native-apdu 906F000000 => 0F9100
exchange native-apdu 9061000000 => 0F009100

# GetFileSettings, the measurement this vector exists for: type 00 Standard
# Data, communication settings 01 MACed, access rights 30 12 on the wire which
# is logical 0x1230, and size 36 00 00 which is 54 octets. Read key 1, write
# key 2, read&write key 3, change key 0, exactly as issued.
exchange native-apdu 90F50000010F00 => 000130123600009100

# An unauthenticated read of the credential. The access rights name read key 1
# and read&write key 3, and no session is open, so the caller is missing an
# authentication rather than being denied outright.
status 90BD0000070F00000000000000 => AE

# GetKeyVersion for a key that does not exist. KS2 declares four keys, so key 04
# is beyond the application's key count and the refusal names that rather than
# reporting a malformed command.
status 906400000104 => 40

# GetFreeMemory, with the credential in place. The card reported E0 1D 00, which
# is 7648 octets free; our engine reported CA 1F 00, which is 8138. This is a
# difference in allocation accounting and granularity rather than in protocol
# behaviour, and the exact figure is a property of the silicon's storage layout,
# so it is recorded as a measurement rather than asserted as an expectation.
#
#   exchange 906E000000 => E01D009100

# GetVersion. The hardware frame matches us byte for byte. In the software
# frame the card reports minor version 04 where we report 03, a known and
# unrelated divergence recorded in hardware_genuine_ev1_4k.vec, so that single
# byte is wildcarded rather than quietly rewritten to ours; everything around it
# stays as measured. The captured byte was 04.
exchange native-apdu 9060000000 => 04010101001A0591AF
exchange native-apdu 90AF000000 => 04010101 ?? 1A0591AF

# The third GetVersion frame carries the UID, then the production batch number
# and the production week and year, which are per-die and which our emulator has
# no way to reproduce. The UID is pinned; the six octets after it are wildcarded.
# The captured frame was 045704EA916F80 B90C214590 1921.
exchange native-apdu 90AF000000 => 045704EA916F80 ?? ?? ?? ?? ?? ?? ?? 9100
